<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened runtime entitlements of cashU.app (scripts/build_macos.sh signs the executable and
  the bundle with them when DEVELOPER_ID_APPLICATION is set). Every exception weakens the signed,
  notarized binary, so only what is shown to be needed is granted (F7 PK4): currently none.
  Evidence and the checklist for adding one back: packaging/README.md ("Entitlements").

  Removed:
  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: needed only by ad-hoc + hardened-runtime
    builds (ad-hoc code has no Team ID, so the embedded Python's libraries "differ"). The
    Developer ID path re-signs every Mach-O file in Frameworks/ and Resources/ with the same
    identity, so library validation passes; disabled, any dylib or .so swapped into the
    user-writable bundle would load into the notarized process.
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory: an ad-hoc + hardened-runtime build
    without it (arm64, macOS 15) opened the window, delivered the token over the pywebview bridge,
    ran the WebKit navigation delegate and the quit handler (pyobjc callbacks, i.e. libffi
    closures), `worker run` and the MCP server.

  Never requested: allow-jit (CPython's JIT is off), allow-dyld-environment-variables (DYLD_*
  injection stays blocked), get-task-allow (no debugger attach), network / file / device
  entitlements (the app is not sandboxed; the server binds 127.0.0.1 only).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
